      { q: "Describe the imp's plan to win over the peasant.", a: "1. He disguised himself as a working man and worked for the peasant.\n2. He advised sowing in a damp low place in a dry year and on a hill in a wet year, so the peasant had a surplus.\n3. He then taught him to make vodka from the extra grain.", marks: 3, type: "short" },
      { q: "How did the peasant behave at his party?", a: "1. He scolded his wife angrily for spilling a drop of drink.\n2. He refused a drink to a poor peasant who came in tired from work.\n3. He drank with his guests and ended up fighting and falling into the mud.", marks: 3, type: "short" },
      { q: "What did the guests become after each glass of vodka?", a: "1. After the first glass they became fox-like, flattering and lying to each other.\n2. After the second they became wolf-like, shouting and fighting.\n3. After the third they became pig-like, making senseless noises and staggering home.", marks: 3, type: "short" },
      { q: "What message does the story give about wealth and moderation?", a: "1. The poor peasant, who lost his bread, remained calm and kind.\n2. When he had grain to spare, he made vodka and became selfish and bad-tempered.\n3. The imp explains that the wild animals are in every man, but remain under control when he has only what he needs.\n4. Surplus without self-control leads to vice.\n5. So the story advises contentment and moderation, showing that wealth is not bad in itself but is harmful when misused.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Write a character sketch of the peasant before and after prosperity.", a: "1. Before: poor, hardworking and honest, he did not curse when his bread was stolen.\n2. He was kind and forgiving, wishing the thief well.\n3. After prosperity: he grew proud and made vodka for pleasure.\n4. He became harsh to his wife and unkind to a poor man who asked for a drink.\n5. He ended up like a drunken beast in the mud, so his change shows how excess corrupts character.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Sameer's father always shared his lunch with the poor watchman when money was short. After he got a big bonus, he began to host parties and gradually grew rude to the servants and stopped helping others.\n(i) Whom does Sameer's father resemble in the story?\n(ii) What does his change show?\n(iii) What lesson does Tolstoy's tale give?", a: "(i) He resembles the peasant, who was kind when poor and turned harsh when he had a surplus.\n(ii) It shows that wealth without self-control can spoil character.\n(iii) We should be content and modest, and remain kind even when we have more than we need.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
